<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jantar Mantar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1724">1724</a>–<a href="/years/1735">1735</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jantar Mantar is the name of the five astronomical observatories built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jai-singh-ii">Sawai Jai Singh II</a> of Amber in the 1720s and 1730s at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>. They consist of huge masonry instruments for measuring time and the positions of celestial bodies. The Jaipur observatory, the largest and best preserved, includes a giant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundial">sundial</a> and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2010.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jantar-mantar.jpg" alt="Jantar Mantar" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Knowledge Seeker · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jantar_Mantar_at_Jaipur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A Jantar Mantar is an assembly of stone-built astronomical instruments designed to be used with the naked eye; each of these mixed instruments, Mishra Yantra, gathers up to 19 different kinds. Among them were the Samrat Yantra, Jai Prakash Yantra, Disha Yantra, Rama Yantra, Chakra Yantra, Rashiwalya Yantra, Dingash Yantra and Utaansh Yantra. Their main purpose was to compile astronomical tables and to predict the times and movements of the Sun, Moon and planets. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jai-singh-ii">Sawai Jai Singh II</a>, who ordered them built, had a keen interest in mathematics, architecture and astronomy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The largest instrument is the Samrat Yantra at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, an equinoctial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundial">sundial</a> and the world's largest stone sundial. It consists of a gigantic triangular gnomon whose hypotenuse lies parallel to the Earth's axis, with a quadrant of a circle on either side parallel to the plane of the equator. A skilled observer can use it with an accuracy of about 20 seconds to find the time of day and the declination of the Sun and other heavenly bodies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The observatory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and the fort that protected it were destroyed before 1857, and it no longer exists; the others stand at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>. The name Jantar Mantar is at least 200 years old, appearing in an account of 1803, though the archives of the Kingdom of Amber of 1735 and 1737–1738 use Jantra instead. Jantar is a spoken form of jantra, from yantra, instrument, and Mantar comes from mantrana, to consult or calculate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jantar Mantar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sawai Jai Singh II founded Jaipur in 1727 on a grid plan and built astronomical observatories, the <b>Jantar Mantar</b>, at Jaipur, Delhi and other cities.</blockquote><a href="/state/jaipur-state">Jaipur State</a></li></ul></section>
